WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.197
A scalar subquery may return zero rows.

00:00:03.197 --> 00:00:07.657
When that happens, PostgreSQL turns the missing scalar into NULL.

00:00:07.657 --> 00:00:11.667
The requested test ID in this example does not exist.

00:00:11.667 --> 00:00:16.837
So the inner query returns no row, and the scalar result becomes NULL.

00:00:16.837 --> 00:00:20.502
Remember this when the scalar participates in a comparison,

00:00:20.502 --> 00:00:24.932
because comparisons involving NULL follow three-valued logic.

00:00:24.932 --> 00:00:28.997
A subquery has a contract: it returns a shape.

00:00:28.997 --> 00:00:33.827
A scalar subquery must return at most one value where it is used.

00:00:33.827 --> 00:00:40.537
Before writing syntax, predict whether the inner query returns one value, one column with many rows,

00:00:40.537 --> 00:00:46.834
or a table. Shape mistakes are easier to prevent than to debug after the query becomes large.
